5.4 Building Lambda Core Engine &amp; API Gateway With our RAG Knowledge Base online, we now build the Serverless Compute Heart of CloudGuard AI: the AWS Lambda Core Audit Engine exposed via a high-throughput Amazon API Gateway (HTTP API).
Step 1: Write Lambda Core Engine Logic (Python 3.12) The Lambda handler executes an automated pipeline:
Ingests raw Terraform HCL from the HTTP POST payload (POST /scan).
Constructs a strict prompt with safety instructions and calls the retrieve_and_generate API in Bedrock Agent Runtime.
Bedrock automatically queries the Knowledge Base, extracts CIS rules, and prompts Claude 3.5 Sonnet to deduce violations.
Parses structured responses: Compliance Score (0-100), severity-graded finding objects, and remediated HCL code.
Persists the audit record into DynamoDB (CloudGuard_ScanHistory).
Emits a clean RESTful JSON response.
Create lambda_function.py:
import json import os import uuid import datetime import boto3 bedrock_agent_runtime = boto3.client(&#39;bedrock-agent-runtime&#39;, region_name=os.environ.get(&#39;AWS_REGION&#39;, &#39;ap-southeast-1&#39;)) dynamodb = boto3.resource(&#39;dynamodb&#39;, region_name=os.environ.get(&#39;AWS_REGION&#39;, &#39;ap-southeast-1&#39;)) KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID = os.environ.get(&#39;KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID&#39;) MODEL_ARN = os.environ.get(&#39;MODEL_ARN&#39;, &#39;arn:aws:bedrock:ap-southeast-1::foundation-model/anthropic.claude-3-5-sonnet-20240620-v1:0&#39;) TABLE_NAME = os.environ.get(&#39;DYNAMODB_TABLE&#39;, &#39;CloudGuard_ScanHistory&#39;) table = dynamodb.Table(TABLE_NAME) def lambda_handler(event, context): try: body = event.get(&#39;body&#39;, {}) if isinstance(body, str): body = json.loads(body) terraform_code = body.get(&#39;terraform_code&#39;, &#39;&#39;) if not terraform_code.strip(): return { &#34;statusCode&#34;: 400, &#34;headers&#34;: {&#34;Content-Type&#34;: &#34;application/json&#34;, &#34;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&#34;: &#34;*&#34;}, &#34;body&#34;: json.dumps({&#34;error&#34;: &#34;Missing &#39;terraform_code&#39; in request payload.&#34;}) } scan_id = str(uuid.uuid4()) scan_time = datetime.datetime.utcnow().isoformat() + &#34;Z&#34; query_prompt = f&#34;&#34;&#34; You are CloudGuard AI, an elite DevSecOps &amp; FinOps infrastructure security auditor. Audit the following Terraform code strictly against the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0 retrieved from the Knowledge Base: ```hcl {terraform_code} Instructions:
Identify all security misconfigurations (e.g. open ports 22/3389 to 0.0.0.0/0, unencrypted S3 buckets, public S3 access, wildcard IAM privileges).
Rate compliance score from 0 (extremely insecure) to 100 (flawless).
Return output strictly as a valid JSON object matching this structure: {{ &ldquo;score&rdquo;: 65, &ldquo;summary&rdquo;: &ldquo;Short executive summary of findings&rdquo;, &ldquo;findings&rdquo;: [ {{ &ldquo;rule_id&rdquo;: &ldquo;CIS-4.1&rdquo;, &ldquo;severity&rdquo;: &ldquo;CRITICAL&rdquo;, &ldquo;resource&rdquo;: &ldquo;aws_security_group.web_sg&rdquo;, &ldquo;description&rdquo;: &ldquo;Port 22 SSH open to 0.0.0.0/0&rdquo;, &ldquo;recommendation&rdquo;: &ldquo;Restrict to internal VPN CIDR.&rdquo; }} ], &ldquo;remediated_code&rdquo;: &ldquo;Full corrected Terraform code snippet&rdquo; }} Return ONLY the raw JSON object, without any markdown backticks or preamble. &quot;&quot;&quot;
response = bedrock_agent_runtime.retrieve_and_generate( input={'text': query_prompt}, retrieveAndGenerateConfiguration={ 'type': 'KNOWLEDGE_BASE', 'knowledgeBaseConfiguration': { 'knowledgeBaseId': KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID, 'modelArn': MODEL_ARN, 'generationConfiguration': { 'inferenceConfig': { 'textInferenceConfig': { 'maxTokens': 2500, 'temperature': 0.1, 'topP': 0.9 } } } } } ) generated_text = response['output']['text'].strip() if generated_text.startswith(&quot;```json&quot;): generated_text = generated_text[7:] if generated_text.startswith(&quot;```&quot;): generated_text = generated_text[3:] if generated_text.endswith(&quot;```&quot;): generated_text = generated_text[:-3] generated_text = generated_text.strip() audit_result = json.loads(generated_text) audit_result['scan_id'] = scan_id audit_result['scanned_at'] = scan_time table.put_item( Item={ 'scan_id': scan_id, 'scanned_at': scan_time, 'score': int(audit_result.get('score', 0)), 'summary': audit_result.get('summary', ''), 'findings_count': len(audit_result.get('findings', [])), 'findings': json.dumps(audit_result.get('findings', [])), 'remediated_code': audit_result.get('remediated_code', '') } ) return { &quot;statusCode&quot;: 200, &quot;headers&quot;: { &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot;, &quot;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&quot;: &quot;*&quot;, &quot;Access-Control-Allow-Methods&quot;: &quot;POST, OPTIONS&quot;, &quot;Access-Control-Allow-Headers&quot;: &quot;Content-Type&quot; }, &quot;body&quot;: json.dumps(audit_result) } except Exception as e: print(f&quot;Error during audit: {str(e)}&quot;) return { &ldquo;statusCode&rdquo;: 500, &ldquo;headers&rdquo;: {&ldquo;Content-Type&rdquo;: &ldquo;application/json&rdquo;, &ldquo;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&rdquo;: &ldquo;*&rdquo;}, &ldquo;body&rdquo;: json.dumps({&ldquo;error&rdquo;: str(e)}) }
2. Package the code into a deployment archive: ```bash zip function.zip lambda_function.py Step 2: Provision the AWS Lambda Function Deploy the serverless function using the AWS CLI: aws lambda create-function \ --function-name cloudguard-core-audit-engine \ --runtime python3.12 \ --role arn:aws:iam::${AWS_ACCOUNT_ID}:role/CloudGuard_Lambda_ExecutionRole \ --handler lambda_function.lambda_handler \ --zip-file fileb://function.zip \ --timeout 60 \ --memory-size 512 \ --environment &#34;Variables={KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID=YOUR_KB_ID_HERE,DYNAMODB_TABLE=CloudGuard_ScanHistory}&#34; \ --region ${AWS_REGION} (Note: Replace YOUR_KB_ID_HERE with your actual Knowledge Base ID from step 5.3).
Step 3: Create Amazon API Gateway (HTTP API) Provision the HTTP API: API_ID=$(aws apigatewayv2 create-api \ --name CloudGuard-API \ --protocol-type HTTP \ --cors-configuration &#34;AllowOrigins=*,AllowMethods=POST,OPTIONS,AllowHeaders=Content-Type&#34; \ --query &#34;ApiId&#34; \ --output text \ --region ${AWS_REGION}) echo &#34;Created API with ID: ${API_ID}&#34; Bind the integration proxy to our Lambda ARN: LAMBDA_ARN=$(aws lambda get-function --function-name cloudguard-core-audit-engine --query &#34;Configuration.FunctionArn&#34; --output text --region ${AWS_REGION}) INTEGRATION_ID=$(aws apigatewayv2 create-integration \ --api-id ${API_ID} \ --integration-type AWS_PROXY \ --integration-uri ${LAMBDA_ARN} \ --payload-format-version &#34;2.0&#34; \ --query &#34;IntegrationId&#34; \ --output text \ --region ${AWS_REGION}) Expose the POST /scan route: aws apigatewayv2 create-route \ --api-id ${API_ID} \ --route-key &#34;POST /scan&#34; \ --target &#34;integrations/${INTEGRATION_ID}&#34; \ --region ${AWS_REGION} Grant invocation permissions to API Gateway: aws lambda add-permission \ --function-name cloudguard-core-audit-engine \ --statement-id &#34;apigateway-invoke-permission&#34; \ --action &#34;lambda:InvokeFunction&#34; \ --principal &#34;apigateway.amazonaws.com&#34; \ --source-arn &#34;arn:aws:execute-api:${AWS_REGION}:${AWS_ACCOUNT_ID}:${API_ID}/*/*/scan&#34; \ --region ${AWS_REGION} Deploy the $default auto-deployment stage: aws apigatewayv2 create-stage \ --api-id ${API_ID} \ --stage-name &#39;$default&#39; \ --auto-deploy \ --region ${AWS_REGION} Your live RESTful endpoint is:
https://${API_ID}.execute-api.${AWS_REGION}.amazonaws.com/scan Step 4: Validate the API with cURL We test the endpoint with a snippet containing 2 severe misconfigurations:
Public SSH port 22 open to 0.0.0.0/0.
Unencrypted S3 bucket.
Execute the curl request:
curl -X POST &#34;https://${API_ID}.execute-api.${AWS_REGION}.amazonaws.com/scan&#34; \ -H &#34;Content-Type: application/json&#34; \ -d &#39;{ &#34;terraform_code&#34;: &#34;resource \&#34;aws_security_group\&#34; \&#34;web\&#34; { name = \&#34;web-sg\&#34; ingress { from_port = 22 to_port = 22 protocol = \&#34;tcp\&#34; cidr_blocks = [\&#34;0.0.0.0/0\&#34;] } }
resource \&#34;aws_s3_bucket\&#34; \&#34;data\&#34; { bucket = \&#34;my-company-confidential-bucket\&#34; }&#34; }&#39; Inspect the synthesized compliance report: { &#34;scan_id&#34;: &#34;c71a39f2-b892-48f5-961d-72fb68912803&#34;, &#34;scanned_at&#34;: &#34;2026-11-09T09:45:00Z&#34;, &#34;score&#34;: 45, &#34;summary&#34;: &#34;Discovered 2 high-severity violations against CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.&#34;, &#34;findings&#34;: [ { &#34;rule_id&#34;: &#34;CIS-4.1&#34;, &#34;severity&#34;: &#34;CRITICAL&#34;, &#34;resource&#34;: &#34;aws_security_group.web&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;Port 22 SSH is open to 0.0.0.0/0, allowing unauthorized remote brute-force attacks.&#34;, &#34;recommendation&#34;: &#34;Restrict inbound traffic to designated corporate CIDR blocks or migrate to AWS Systems Manager Session Manager.&#34; }, { &#34;rule_id&#34;: &#34;CIS-2.1.1&#34;, &#34;severity&#34;: &#34;HIGH&#34;, &#34;resource&#34;: &#34;aws_s3_bucket.data&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;Default server-side encryption is absent on S3 bucket.&#34;, &#34;recommendation&#34;: &#34;Attach aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration with SSE-S3 or KMS.&#34; } ], &#34;remediated_code&#34;: &#34;resource \&#34;aws_security_group\&#34; \&#34;web\&#34; {
 name = \&#34;web-sg\&#34;
 ingress {
 from_port = 22
 to_port = 22
 protocol = \&#34;tcp\&#34;
 cidr_blocks = [\&#34;10.0.0.0/16\&#34;]
 }
}

resource \&#34;aws_s3_bucket\&#34; \&#34;data\&#34; {
 bucket = \&#34;my-company-confidential-bucket\&#34;
}

resource \&#34;aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration\&#34; \&#34;data_enc\&#34; {
 bucket = aws_s3_bucket.data.id
 rule {
 apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
 sse_algorithm = \&#34;AES256\&#34;
 }
 }
}&#34; } Confirm persistence in DynamoDB: aws dynamodb scan --table-name CloudGuard_ScanHistory --max-items 1 --region ${AWS_REGION} The core serverless engine is operating with 100% precision. Next, we build the Interactive Web Dashboard and EventBridge Automated Scheduler!

5.6 End-to-End Testing &amp; Resource Cleanup Congratulations on reaching the final module of the CloudGuard AI workshop series! In this capstone section, you will:
Execute an End-to-End Validation Scenario featuring an enterprise Terraform template injected with multiple real-world vulnerabilities. Verify the Integrated Lifecycle: HCL parsing -&gt; Bedrock RAG retrieval -&gt; Claude 3.5 Sonnet reasoning -&gt; Posture scoring -&gt; Code auto-remediation -&gt; DynamoDB persistence -&gt; Emergency SNS email alert. Perform a Complete Resource Teardown (Clean up) to guarantee you incur zero lingering charges on your AWS account. Step 1: Comprehensive End-to-End Test Scenario We assemble a realistic Terraform script containing 4 catastrophic security misconfigurations:
CIS-4.1 Violation: Security Group opening SSH port 22 to 0.0.0.0/0.
CIS-2.1.1 Violation: S3 bucket lacking server-side encryption (SSE).
CIS-2.1.2 Violation: S3 bucket lacking Public Access Block controls.
CIS-1.16 Violation: Overly permissive IAM policy with wildcard (&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;*&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;).
Create vulnerable_infrastructure.tf:
# 1. Dangerous public SSH port 22 exposure resource &#34;aws_security_group&#34; &#34;bastion_host&#34; { name = &#34;bastion-sg&#34; description = &#34;Public Bastion Host Security Group&#34; vpc_id = &#34;vpc-0123456789abcdef0&#34; ingress { from_port = 22 to_port = 22 protocol = &#34;tcp&#34; cidr_blocks = [&#34;0.0.0.0/0&#34;] # CIS-4.1 VIOLATION } } # 2. Unencrypted, public-ready S3 Bucket resource &#34;aws_s3_bucket&#34; &#34;financial_reports&#34; { bucket = &#34;company-financial-reports-2026&#34; # CIS-2.1.1 (No encryption) &amp; CIS-2.1.2 (No Public Access Block) } # 3. Excessive IAM administrative policy resource &#34;aws_iam_policy&#34; &#34;dev_admin&#34; { name = &#34;dev-admin-policy&#34; description = &#34;Excessive developer privileges&#34; policy = jsonencode({ Version = &#34;2012-10-17&#34; Statement = [ { Effect = &#34;Allow&#34; Action = &#34;*&#34; # CIS-1.16 VIOLATION Resource = &#34;*&#34; } ] }) } Submit the payload to your CloudGuard API Gateway endpoint: TF_CONTENT=$(cat vulnerable_infrastructure.tf | python -c &#34;import json, sys; print(json.dumps(sys.stdin.read()))&#34;) curl -X POST &#34;https://${API_ID}.execute-api.${AWS_REGION}.amazonaws.com/scan&#34; \ -H &#34;Content-Type: application/json&#34; \ -d &#34;{&#34;terraform_code&#34;: ${TF_CONTENT}}&#34; Step 2: Analyze Audit Synthesis The JSON response showcases contextual accuracy that far exceeds legacy static linters:
{ &#34;scan_id&#34;: &#34;9e24a1b8-6d20-410a-b271-93c4e0987114&#34;, &#34;scanned_at&#34;: &#34;2026-11-09T09:50:15Z&#34;, &#34;score&#34;: 25, &#34;summary&#34;: &#34;CRITICAL RISK: Found 4 severe misconfigurations violating CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0. Deployment halted.&#34;, &#34;findings&#34;: [ { &#34;rule_id&#34;: &#34;CIS-4.1&#34;, &#34;severity&#34;: &#34;CRITICAL&#34;, &#34;resource&#34;: &#34;aws_security_group.bastion_host&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;Port 22 SSH is open to the public internet (0.0.0.0/0), exposing instance to brute-force credential stuffing.&#34;, &#34;recommendation&#34;: &#34;Restrict ingress to internal corporate CIDR (/16 or /24) or use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager.&#34; }, { &#34;rule_id&#34;: &#34;CIS-1.16&#34;, &#34;severity&#34;: &#34;CRITICAL&#34;, &#34;resource&#34;: &#34;aws_iam_policy.dev_admin&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;IAM Policy grants full administrative privileges (*) across all resources, violating Principle of Least Privilege.&#34;, &#34;recommendation&#34;: &#34;Enumerate granular service actions and define specific ARNs in Resource block.&#34; }, { &#34;rule_id&#34;: &#34;CIS-2.1.1&#34;, &#34;severity&#34;: &#34;HIGH&#34;, &#34;resource&#34;: &#34;aws_s3_bucket.financial_reports&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;Default server-side encryption is not enforced on the S3 bucket.&#34;, &#34;recommendation&#34;: &#34;Define aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration with AES256 or KMS.&#34; }, { &#34;rule_id&#34;: &#34;CIS-2.1.2&#34;, &#34;severity&#34;: &#34;CRITICAL&#34;, &#34;resource&#34;: &#34;aws_s3_bucket.financial_reports&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;S3 Public Access Block configuration is missing, allowing potential public exposure of financial data.&#34;, &#34;recommendation&#34;: &#34;Attach aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block with all 4 guardrails enabled.&#34; } ], &#34;remediated_code&#34;: &#34;# REMEDIATED BY CLOUDGUARD AI (CLAUDE 3.5 SONNET)

resource &#34;aws_security_group&#34; &#34;bastion_host&#34; {
 name = &#34;bastion-sg&#34;
 description = &#34;Secure Bastion Host Security Group&#34;
 vpc_id = &#34;vpc-0123456789abcdef0&#34;

 ingress {
 from_port = 22
 to_port = 22
 protocol = &#34;tcp&#34;
 cidr_blocks = [&#34;10.0.0.0/16&#34;] # Fixed: restricted to internal VPC
 }
}

resource &#34;aws_s3_bucket&#34; &#34;financial_reports&#34; {
 bucket = &#34;company-financial-reports-2026&#34;
}

resource &#34;aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration&#34; &#34;financial_reports_enc&#34; {
 bucket = aws_s3_bucket.financial_reports.id
 rule {
 apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
 sse_algorithm = &#34;AES256&#34;
 }
 }
}

resource &#34;aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block&#34; &#34;financial_reports_pab&#34; {
 bucket = aws_s3_bucket.financial_reports.id
 block_public_acls = true
 block_public_policy = true
 ignore_public_acls = true
 restrict_public_buckets = true
}

resource &#34;aws_iam_policy&#34; &#34;dev_admin&#34; {
 name = &#34;dev-scoped-policy&#34;
 description = &#34;Scoped developer permissions following Least Privilege&#34;

 policy = jsonencode({
 Version = &#34;2012-10-17&#34;
 Statement = [
 {
 Effect = &#34;Allow&#34;
 Action = [&#34;ec2:Describe*&#34;, &#34;s3:ListBucket&#34;, &#34;s3:GetObject&#34;]
 Resource = &#34;*&#34;
 }
 ]
 })
}&#34; } Verify Urgent Email Alert: Check the email inbox subscribed in Step 5.5: You will see the incoming alert with subject: [URGENT] CloudGuard Security Violation Detected The body accurately reports the 25/100 score and enumerates discovered critical findings! Step 3: Complete Resource Teardown (Clean Up) To eliminate any potential AWS billing after completing this educational project, execute the following teardown commands:
1. Delete EventBridge Schedule: aws scheduler delete-schedule --name &#34;CloudGuard-DailyPosturedAudit&#34; --region ${AWS_REGION} 2. Delete Amazon API Gateway: aws apigatewayv2 delete-api --api-id ${API_ID} --region ${AWS_REGION} 3. Delete AWS Lambda Function &amp; CloudWatch Log Group: aws lambda delete-function --function-name cloudguard-core-audit-engine --region ${AWS_REGION} aws logs delete-log-group --log-group-name &#34;/aws/lambda/cloudguard-core-audit-engine&#34; --region ${AWS_REGION} 4. Delete Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base &amp; Vector Index: Open Amazon Bedrock Console -&gt; Knowledge bases. Select CloudGuard-CIS-KnowledgeBase and click Delete. Navigate to Amazon OpenSearch Service -&gt; Serverless: Collections, delete the collection provisioned during knowledge base setup. 5. Delete Amazon DynamoDB Table: aws dynamodb delete-table --table-name CloudGuard_ScanHistory --region ${AWS_REGION} 6. Purge Objects &amp; Delete S3 Bucket: aws s3 rm s3://cloudguard-audit-data-${AWS_ACCOUNT_ID} --recursive aws s3 rb s3://cloudguard-audit-data-${AWS_ACCOUNT_ID} --force --region ${AWS_REGION} 7. Delete Amazon SNS Topic: aws sns delete-topic --topic-arn ${SNS_TOPIC_ARN} --region ${AWS_REGION} 8. Delete IAM Roles &amp; Policies: aws iam delete-role-policy --role-name CloudGuard_Lambda_ExecutionRole --policy-name CloudGuard_Core_Policy aws iam delete-role --role-name CloudGuard_Lambda_ExecutionRole aws iam delete-role-policy --role-name CloudGuard_Scheduler_Role --policy-name InvokeLambdaPolicy aws iam delete-role --role-name CloudGuard_Scheduler_Role Workshop Summary Congratulations on successfully building CloudGuard AI!
Through this hands-on workshop, you have mastered mission-critical skills at the intersection of Cloud Engineering, Generative AI, and DevSecOps:
Production GenAI &amp; RAG Engineering: Structuring regulatory standards into high-dimension vector stores and guiding Claude 3.5 Sonnet to perform deterministic compliance reasoning without hallucination. Shift-Left Security Philosophy: Preventing misconfigurations before deployment by auditing IaC templates early in development cycles. Event-Driven Serverless Mastery: Architecting an automated, zero-idle-cost auditing pipeline compliant with the AWS Well-Architected Framework.
